782
00:51:15,440 --> 00:51:19,260
It has basically just built a chaotic, tangled mess of highly specific spikes.

783
00:51:19,500 --> 00:51:20,460
So it's just cheating.

784
00:51:20,700 --> 00:51:28,100
It's like a student cramming for a math test by just memorizing the answer key without actually understanding how the formulas work.

785
00:51:28,180 --> 00:51:28,520
Exactly.

786
00:51:28,740 --> 00:51:33,560
Under classical rules, you would stop the experiment right there and say the model is irreparably overfit.

787
00:51:33,560 --> 00:51:35,400
But here's the crazy part.

788
00:51:36,140 --> 00:51:47,560
If the researchers ignore the rules and just leave the AI running if they keep optimizing it on the exact same training data long after the error has already hit zero, something magical happens.

789
00:51:47,680 --> 00:51:48,000
What happens?

790
00:51:48,160 --> 00:51:51,680
Eventually, suddenly, the generalization performance skyrockets.

791
00:51:52,220 --> 00:51:54,540
The model abruptly groks the underlying rule.

792
00:51:54,660 --> 00:51:54,880
Wait.

793
00:51:55,080 --> 00:51:57,320
It figures out the rule after it memorized all the answers.

794
00:51:57,320 --> 00:52:03,520
So basically, grokking is the AI building all the messy, chaotic spikes first just to pass the test.

795
00:52:03,840 --> 00:52:08,160
And then slowly, over time, it naturally paves the smooth highway underneath them.

796
00:52:08,360 --> 00:52:09,880
That is exactly what is happening.

797
00:52:10,160 --> 00:52:12,800
The paper calls this delayed manifold organization.

798
00:52:12,800 --> 00:52:16,120
The model satisfies the strict constraints locally.

799
00:52:16,560 --> 00:52:18,160
First, it builds the spikes.

